IN-PROCESS RECOMPRESSION FEASIBILITY PROBE
==========================================

Question: can the plugin, in-process, gzip the exact official tar back to the
byte-exact official .app.tar.gz? This is the gate for the tar-layer strategy,
because the minisign signature covers the compressed artifact and Update::install
consumes it.

The controlled tar-layer experiment established exact recompression only by
running the installed cargo-tauri 2.10.1 binary over an extracted tree. Its own
scope section lists "standalone raw-tar recompression stability" as NOT
established. A shipped plugin cannot invoke the bundler, so that gap is decisive.

Input:  /private/tmp/delta-e2e-build/v1.0.1/DeltaUpdaterExample.app.tar.gz
        official compressed artifact, 4070756 bytes
        exact tar via gzip -dc, 9461248 bytes

Method: flate2::write::GzEncoder::new(Vec::new(), Compression::default()),
        single write_all of the exact tar, finish(), compare to official.
        This mirrors tauri-bundler's documented create_tar construction.

Results:
  flate2 1.1.9 + zlib-rs 0.6.7      candidate 4070510  DIFFERENT at byte 47
  flate2 1.1.9 + zlib-rs 0.6.3      candidate 4070510  DIFFERENT at byte 47
    (0.6.3 is the exact version the controlled experiment identified)
  flate2 1.1.9 + system zlib (C)    candidate 4070510  DIFFERENT at byte 47
  flate2 1.1.9 + miniz_oxide 0.8.9  candidate 4070510  DIFFERENT at byte 47

Observations:
  - All four backends produce byte-identical output to each other.
  - All four differ from the official at the same offset, byte 47, which is
    37 bytes into the DEFLATE stream. The 10-byte gzip header matches
    (1f 8b 08 00 00000000 00 ff): mtime 0, XFL 0, OS 255. No filename field.
  - Every candidate is 246 bytes SHORTER than the official.

Interpretation:
  The cause is NOT the DEFLATE backend. Four independent implementations agree
  with each other and disagree with the official at one early offset, and the
  official is consistently larger. That is the signature of a different
  write/flush pattern into the encoder rather than a different encoder: a
  one-shot compression of the whole tar packs marginally better than a stream
  interrupted at boundaries.

  The likely mechanism is that tauri-bundler writes the tar into the GzEncoder
  incrementally through tar::Builder as it walks the tree, so DEFLATE sees entry
  boundaries. HYPOTHESIS, not established: tauri-bundler's source is not vendored
  here (cargo-tauri is installed as a binary), so the write pattern was not read.

Conclusion:
  Byte-exact in-process recompression is NOT demonstrated and the obvious recipe
  does not work. Stop condition triggered.
